          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code copies a string into a destination buffer without verifying that the destination pointer is not null. The source is a C-style string, which is generally safe from overlap issues, but the analysis indicates that the destination pointer may be null at this point in the code, and there is no evidence of a guard or check to prevent this. If the destination pointer is null, calling the copy function will result in undefined behavior, potentially causing a crash or other unpredictable results. There is no explicit null-termination after the copy, but the main risk here is the lack of a null check for the destination pointer.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the destination pointer (`cpu_info`) and its `model_name` member are not null. This prevents undefined behavior if the pointer is invalid. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (cpu_info != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(cpu_info->model_name, model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    cpu_info->model_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\nThis check ensures that the destination buffer is valid before performing the copy. Additionally, explicitly null-terminating the buffer after the copy is a good practice to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-cpp-secure-coding-practices/)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n\n___\n"
