            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function (strncpy) where the source and destination buffers may overlap, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Overlapping memory regions in such operations can lead to undefined behavior, which may cause unpredictable program behavior or security issues. The source and destination are both identifiers, and there is no evidence that they are guaranteed to be non-overlapping. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, and the source pointer may be null at this point, increasing the risk. No guard or capacity check is present to mitigate these risks.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior from overlapping source and destination buffers in string copy operations, ensure that the source and destination do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Additionally, always ensure the destination buffer is null-terminated after the copy to prevent string-related vulnerabilities.\n\nExample remediation:\n\n```cpp\n// If overlap is possible, use memmove and ensure null-termination\nmemmove(info->version, vbios_version, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\ninfo->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you can guarantee that the source and destination do not overlap, document this assumption clearly in the code and add an assertion:\n\n```cpp\n// Assert non-overlapping buffers (if you can guarantee it)\nassert(info->version != vbios_version);\nstrncpy(info->version, vbios_version, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\ninfo->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n- [CWE-119: 