          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function (`strncpy`) where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard, as overlapping memory regions are not allowed for this function. The analysis detected possible overlap through data flow analysis, and there are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this. Additionally, the source buffer may be null, which could also cause runtime errors. No evidence was found that the destination buffer is null, but the potential for source/destination overlap is a significant risk. The destination buffer's capacity is not explicitly known, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which further increases the risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the risk of undefined behavior from overlapping source and destination buffers in the use of `strncpy`, ensure that the source and destination do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. For example, replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->version, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.vbios_ver_str), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n```\n\nAdditionally, if you require null-termination of the destination buffer, explicitly set the last byte to `\\0` after the copy:\n\n```cpp\ninfo->version[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\n\n___\n"
