          "message": {
            "text": "Problematic C function detected (memcpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis detected a possible overlap between the source and destination buffers in a memory copy operation. The code uses a memory copy function to transfer data from one buffer to another, but dataflow analysis suggests that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C++ standard, as memory copy functions like memcpy do not support overlapping regions. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the destination buffer's capacity is not known or enforced. Both the source and destination are confirmed to be non-null, so the main risk is the potential for undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between buffers that may overlap, use `std::memmove` instead of `std::memcpy`. The `std::memmove` function is specifically designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. Replace the memory copy function in your code with `std::memmove`:\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(cpu_set, bitmask.data(), cpu_set_size * sizeof(uint64_t));\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination buffers overlap. For more information, see the [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices/) and [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html).\n\n___\n"
          },
