            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function (strncpy) where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard, which prohibits overlapping memory regions for functions like strncpy. The analysis detected a feasible data flow from the destination to the source, indicating a real risk of overlap. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to prevent this, and the source may be null, further increasing the risk. The destination buffer's capacity is not explicitly known, and the count argument is not directly tied to the destination's size, which adds to the uncertainty. While the source is not a string literal or a constant string (which would reduce the risk of overlap), the presence of a NUL terminator assignment after the call does not mitigate the undefined behavior risk from overlapping buffers.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate the undefined behavior risk from possible overlapping source and destination buffers in the strncpy call, ensure that the source and destination do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use memmove instead of strncpy, as memmove is defined for overlapping memory regions. For example, replace the call with:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(info->build_date, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\ninfo->build_date[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you can guarantee that the source and destination never overlap, document this assumption clearly in the code and add an assertion to enforce it:\n\n```cpp\nassert(info->build_date != reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date));\nstrncpy(info->build_date, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.date), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\ninfo->build_date[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nFor more information, see [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow) and [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html).\n\n___\n"
