            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function (strncpy) where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. This can lead to undefined behavior according to the C standard, which prohibits overlapping memory regions for this function. The analysis detected a feasible data flow path where the destination could be derived from the source, increasing the risk of overlap. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination of the destination buffer after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. There are no guards or checks ensuring that the source and destination do not overlap, and the source pointer may also be null at this point, which could cause a crash. These factors all contribute to this being a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before calling the string copy function. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is safe for overlapping memory regions. Additionally, always explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to avoid unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\n// Before copying, check for overlap or use memmove if overlap is possible\nif (info->vram_vendor != brand) {\n    strncpy(info->vram_vendor, brand, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    info->vram_vendor[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n} else {\n    // If overlap is possible, use memmove\n    memmove(info->vram_vendor, brand, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    info->vram_vendor[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitio