          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses the strncpy function to copy data from one buffer to another, but there is a risk that the source and destination buffers may overlap. Overlapping memory regions with strncpy leads to undefined behavior, which can cause unpredictable program behavior or security vulnerabilities. The analysis detected that the source and destination may be related through data flow, increasing the risk of overlap. Additionally, there is no evidence of explicit null-termination after the copy, and the source pointer may be null at this point, further increasing the risk. The destination buffer's capacity is unknown, and there is no guard to ensure safe copying. All these factors contribute to the high confidence in this being a real issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior caused by overlapping source and destination buffers in strncpy, ensure that the source and destination do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use memmove instead of strncpy, as memmove is designed to handle overlapping memory regions safely. For example, replace the original call with:\n\n```cpp\nif (name != proc_id) {\n    strncpy(name, proc_id, len);\n} else {\n    // If overlap is possible, use memmove\n    memmove(name, proc_id, len);\n}\n```\n\nAlternatively, if you know the buffers never overlap, add an explicit assertion or comment to document this assumption:\n\n```cpp\nassert(name != proc_id); // Ensure no overlap\nstrncpy(name, proc_id, len);\n```\n\nAlways ensure that the destination buffer is large enough for the copy and that the source pointer is not null before copying. Consider adding null checks and buffer size validations as appropriate.\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definiti