          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy operation (`strncpy`) where the destination buffer or the source pointer may be null, and there are no visible checks to ensure they are valid before the operation. The source is a C-style string derived from a C++ string object, which is generally safe, but the destination buffer (`info.name`) could potentially be null if not properly initialized or allocated. The absence of explicit null checks for both the source and destination increases the risk of undefined behavior, such as a segmentation fault or program crash. The analysis did not detect any guards or capacity checks that would mitigate this risk. Therefore, this is a genuine issue that should be addressed to ensure program stability and security.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the string copy operation, ensure that the destination buffer (`info.name`) is not null. Add an explicit check to prevent undefined behavior if the buffer is uninitialized or invalid. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (info.name != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(info.name, name.c_str(), std::min((unsigned long)AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH, name.length()));\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination:\n    info.name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis check prevents the program from attempting to write to a null pointer, which would otherwise result in a crash. Additionally, consider always ensuring the destination buffer is properly allocated and initialized before use.\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n\n___\n"
