          "ruleId": "DS161085",
          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Problematic C function detected (malloc)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of malloc is used as the destination in a memory operation (such as strcpy) without a preceding check for NULL. The absence of a null guard before the first use of the allocated pointer increases the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The use of a cast does not mitigate this risk. The allocation size is not a simple sizeof(T) pattern, and there are no zero or overflow guards present. The pointer is returned from the function and also passed to another call, but these do not reduce the risk of dereferencing a null pointer. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before using the allocated pointer. If malloc returns NULL, handle the error appropriately (e.g., return an error code, set an error status, or clean up resources). For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\ngpu_vendor_name = (char*)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nstrcpy(gpu_vendor_name, src);\n```\n\nA safe remediation would be:\n\n```c\ngpu_vendor_name = (char*)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nif (gpu_vendor_name == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure (e.g., return error, log, etc.)\n    return ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY;\n}\nstrcpy(gpu_vendor_name, src);\n```\n\nThis ensures that strcpy is only called if the allocation succeeded, preventing a possible null pointer dereference. For more information, see [OWASP C Memory Management](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Improper_Handling_of_Insufficient_Memory_Conditions) and [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html).\n\n___\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
