          "ruleId": "DS161085",
          "kind": "fail",
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Problematic C function detected (malloc)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of malloc is used as the destination in a memory operation (such as strcpy) without a preceding check for NULL. The absence of a null guard before the first use increases the risk of a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The use of a cast does not mitigate this risk. The allocation size is not a simple literal or sizeof(T) pattern, and there are no zero or overflow guards present. The pointer is returned from the function and also passed to another call, but these do not reduce the risk of dereferencing a null pointer. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a null pointer dereference after a failed memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nvbios_version = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nstrcpy(vbios_version, ...);\n```\n\nYou should add a null check immediately after the allocation:\n\n```c\nvbios_version = (char *)malloc(sizeof(char)*len);\nif (vbios_version == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return an error or clean up\n    return ERROR_CODE;\n}\nstrcpy(vbios_version, ...);\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would lead to undefined behavior or a crash.\n\nFor more information, see:\n- [OWASP C Secure Coding Practices: Memory Allocation](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/#memory-allocation)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
