          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is classified as high risk because the destination buffer's capacity is not known at this point in the code, and the number of bytes to copy is not proven to be within the bounds of the destination buffer. The code copies data from a C-style string into a destination buffer using a length argument that is not directly tied to the actual size of the destination. There is no evidence of a guard that checks whether the length is within the buffer's capacity, nor is there an explicit null-terminator assignment after the copy. While the source is a C-style string (which reduces the risk of source/destination overlap), the main concern is the potential for buffer overflow or incomplete null-termination if the length exceeds the actual buffer size. This can lead to memory corruption or security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate this issue, ensure that the number of bytes copied does not exceed the actual size of the destination buffer. If possible, use a constant or a value derived from the actual buffer size. Additionally, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated strings.\n\nFor example, if you know the size of `version` (e.g., `VERSION_SIZE`), use:\n\n```cpp\nstd::strncpy(version, empty.c_str(), VERSION_SIZE - 1);\nversion[VERSION_SIZE - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIf the size is dynamic, ensure that `len` is always less than or equal to the buffer's capacity, and always null-terminate:\n\n```cpp\nif (len > 0 && len <= buffer_capacity) {\n    std::strncpy(version, empty.c_str(), len - 1);\n    version[len - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nRefer to the following resources for more information:\n- [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\n___\n"
