          "message": {
            "text": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
            "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the destination buffer's actual capacity is not known at this point in the code, and the number of bytes to copy is determined by an external variable (sizeOfBuff-1) that is not proven to be safe. The use of strncpy with a destination pointer and an unverified size can lead to buffer overflows if sizeOfBuff exceeds the actual allocated size of the buffer. Although the source is a C++ string's c_str() (which is safe from overlap and null pointer issues), the main risk is that the code does not guarantee that the destination buffer is large enough for the specified copy size. This is a common source of memory corruption vulnerabilities in C/C++ code.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo remediate this issue, ensure that the value used for the copy size does not exceed the actual allocated size of the destination buffer. If the buffer's size is known at compile time, use `sizeof(buff)` to determine the maximum safe copy size. If the buffer is dynamically allocated, ensure that the size variable accurately reflects the allocation. For example, if `buff` is a local array:\n\n```cpp\n// If buff is declared as: char buff[256];\nstd::strncpy(buff, contents.c_str(), sizeof(buff) - 1);\nbuff[sizeof(buff) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nIf the buffer is allocated dynamically, make sure `sizeOfBuff` is set to the allocated size and is never larger than the actual buffer. Always validate the size before copying:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeOfBuff > 0 && sizeOfBuff <= actual_buffer_size) {\n    std::strncpy(buff, contents.c_str(), sizeOfBuff - 1);\n    buff[sizeOfBuff - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nFor more information, see [OWASP C Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow) and [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html).\n\n___\n"
